{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-34725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-34725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-34725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f4606f455d7c2010df6faad4bf61acfb47eeffe46efa039bf87239956ed746e"} {"question" "q-34725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_wid