nd{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-35299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f4606f455d7c2010df6faad4bf61acfb47eeffe46efa039bf87239956ed746e"} {"question" "q-35299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-35299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-35299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]})=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f4606f455d7c2010df6faad4bf61acfb47eeffe46efa039bf87239956ed746e"} {"question" "q-79313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f4606f455d7c2010df6faad4bf61acfb47eeffe46efa039bf87239956ed746e"} {"question" "q-79319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79326" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w})=5$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79326" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071804/state.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba1637bb1f17165363bd489182221b9a080a098500239e311faf30517fcec9"} {"question" "q-79326" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" 